 # Automatic and Data-Driven Generation
 
-Also called: Automatic Transfer Function Design, Data-Driven Classification, TF Generation
+Also called: Automated Transfer Function Design, Automatic TF Generation
 
 ## Overview
-Automatic and Data-Driven Generation methods produce volume rendering transfer functions (TFs) without manual user interaction, solving the labor-intensive and subjective process of manually tuning opacity and color mappings for complex volumetric data [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. Unlike manual design or simple histogram-based thresholding, these methods leverage statistical analysis, optimization loops, or machine learning models to derive optical properties from the data's intrinsic features or external examples [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. This family encompasses approaches ranging from heuristic feature extraction (e.g., gradient-based boundaries) to learned mappings (e.g., GANs, diffusion models) that synthesize TFs or entire rendered images conditioned on view and data properties [S1, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
+Automatic and Data-Driven Generation methods derive volume rendering transfer functions (TFs) algorithmically from the input data or external examples, eliminating the need for manual user interaction [S1, S2, S3, S4, unofficial]. This family solves the labor-intensive, subjective, and time-consuming process of manually tuning opacity and color mappings for complex volumetric datasets [S1, S2, S3, unofficial]. What sets this family apart from its siblings (such as [Interaction and Editing Interfaces](6592-interaction-and-editing-interfaces.md) or manual [1D Transfer Function](11719-1d-transfer-function.md) design) is the reliance on statistical analysis, optimization loops, machine learning models, or geometric properties to determine classification parameters automatically [S1, S2, S3, S4, unofficial].
 
