 ## Background
-Automatic transfer function generation emerged as a response to the complexity of manual TF design, particularly for multidimensional data and large datasets. Early methods focused on histogram analysis and peak detection to identify material boundaries [S2, unofficial]. As data complexity increased, methods incorporating gradient and derivative information became prevalent to distinguish overlapping scalar ranges [S3, unofficial]. More recently, machine learning approaches, including clustering, optimization, and generative adversarial networks (GANs), have been adopted to learn mappings from data features or example images to optimal visualizations [S1, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. The field continues to evolve with the integration of deep learning architectures like transformers and diffusion models for super-resolution and synthesis tasks [S3, unofficial].
+Automatic transfer function generation emerged as a response to the complexity and subjectivity of manual TF design, particularly for large-scale or multidimensional scientific data. Early approaches focused on histogram analysis to identify peaks and valleys corresponding to material boundaries [S3, unofficial]. Subsequent developments introduced clustering techniques, such as Gaussian Mixture Models (GMM) and Valley Cell-Based Clustering, to partition the feature space automatically [S1, S2, S3, unofficial]. More recently, the field has shifted towards data-driven and learning-based methods, including Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) for image synthesis [S1, unofficial] and Diffusion Models for handling multimodal distributions [S4, unofficial]. These methods are widely used in medical imaging (e.g., CT, MRI, DBT) and scientific visualization to accelerate exploration and standardize visualization pipelines [S1, S2, S3, S4, unofficial].
 
